task.body:
`bounded()` in `internal/messagelifecycle/reconstruct.go` truncates recipient/review diagnostic text (`smtp_detail`, `failure_reason`, `failure_code`, `review_resolution`) at a raw byte offset with no UTF-8 boundary check:

```go
func bounded(value string) string {
	if len(value) <= maxDiagnosticStringBytes {
		return value
	}
	return value[:maxDiagnosticStringBytes]
}
```

The sibling function `SafeDiagnostic()` in `model.go` does the same job for a different call site and gets it right, extending the truncation back to a valid rune boundary. `bounded()` predates it and was never updated to match.

Since `message_recipients.detail` holds live SMTP bounce/deferral text from foreign mail servers, a diagnostic that happens to land a multi-byte rune across byte 2048 gets cut mid-rune. `validatedEvidenceCopy` (model.go) only checks `len(text) > maxDiagnosticStringBytes`, not UTF-8 validity, so the invalid string is accepted, and the JSON round-trip in `cloneTransition` silently substitutes U+FFFD for the broken bytes rather than erroring.

Repro (Go, package `messagelifecycle`): feed a `RecipientSnapshot.Detail` of 683 repeats of `中` (2049 bytes, so the 2048-byte cap always splits the last rune) through `Reconstruct()`. The resulting `Evidence["smtp_detail"]` comes back invalid UTF-8 (and, after the JSON round-trip, longer than the 2048-byte cap it was supposed to enforce).

Fix is to route `bounded()` through `SafeDiagnostic()` so both call sites share one truncation rule.
